Amanda: quick sync — i've put a lightweight PRD draft in the doc. primary ask: firm up launch gates for structured output + tool calling. customer-driven items (enterprise X & Y) pushed stricter validation requirements. need acceptance criteria + canary plan by EOD tomorrow. :hourglass: 

Rob: ack. can you list the non-negotiables you have so far?

Amanda: sure — top-level: 1) runtime schema validation that fails safe (falls back to text-only with telemetry), 2) defined error codes for fn/tool failures, 3) backward-compatible schema evolution rules, 4) perf SLAs: p95 <= 220ms for standard 1k-token chat with function calls, 5) telemetry hooks for malformed outputs and fn invocations.

Jia: for ML side — runtime validation should be configurable per-route. some customers want strict validation (reject if schema mismatch), others want permissive (auto-repair heuristics + fallback). we should support both modes and surface mismatch diffs in traces.

Sarah: QA: acceptance criteria suggestions: - unit test suite with synthetic prompts that exercise edgecases (missing fields, extra fields, wrong types) - golden prompt regression checks for 12 representative flows - chaos tests where tool returns corrupted JSON - automatic post-deploy validation that samples 1% of traffic and runs full schema check.

Omar: devex + SDKs: we need consistent error shapes across SDKs. propose an error contract: { code, message, details[], suggestion } and a client-side helper to map to typed exceptions. also lightweight sample code for retry/backoff semantics.

Nora: security: ensure we don't log PII in full when capturing malformed payloads. capture hashes + minimal context. also require KMS usage for any stored telemetry blobs and RBAC for who can read raw outputs.

Amanda: good. i'm adding these into the PRD. two outstanding questions: a) how strict is the canary? fail open or fail closed for first canary? b) what are acceptable fallback policies (model variant vs text-only vs partial tool output)?

Rob: propose fail open for the first canary (so customer traffic isn't dropped), but we must emit a high-severity alert and throttle routing to the canary group if malformed rate > 1%. have an automatic rollback to previous model variant after X mins if error spike persists.

Jia: fallback policy matrix idea: - schema minor version mismatch -> attempt auto-correction heuristics (field coercion, defaulting) + flag - schema major version mismatch -> route to text-only path + emit telemetry - tool execution error -> surface structured error to caller + provide raw payload on request (restricted).

Sarah: QA gating: require passing of all unit + regression tests AND <0.5% malformed outputs in synthetic stress tests. if >0.5% but <2% allow canary with mitigations and nightly triage. >2% = no go.

Omar: rollout plan: 1) internal dogfood (10% of internal traffic), 2) opt-in enterprise partners (2 customers), 3) broader canary (10% of prod), 4) general availability. each stage has explicit telemetry dashboards and a small playbook for rollback.

Amanda: good. acceptance criteria to put in PRD (draft): - feature flags for strict/permissive validation per-route - documented error contract across SDKs - telemetry for malformed: rate, sample of raw (redacted), top 10 offending prompts - performance: p95 <= 220ms with feature on for 90% of traffic - regression harness + 12 golden flows - canary gating thresholds (QA numbers from Sarah) - security controls for telemetry access.

Rob: technical notes for the spec (paste into RFC): ```
// runtime validation hook (pseudocode)
onResponse(response, schema, routeConfig) {
  try {
    validate(response, schema);
    emitMetric('schema.valid', 1);
    return response;
  } catch (err) {
    emitMetric('schema.invalid', 1);
    if (routeConfig.validationMode === 'strict') throw formatError(err);
    // permissive: attempt auto-fix
    const fixed = attemptAutoFix(response, schema);
    if (fixed.ok) return fixed.value;
    // fallback: strip structured wrapper and surface text
    return { text: extractText(response), _fallback: true };
  }
}
```

Jia: include KV cache implications: if we change schema shape we must avoid caching incompatible entries. add cache key versioning tied to schema version.

Nora: also add compliance checklist: data retention policy for samples, delete window, audit log for access.

Amanda: i've updated the doc with the draft ACs + canary steps. next: Rob & Jia to draft RFC section on runtime behavior and fallback matrix; Sarah to write the tests matrix; Omar to draft SDK error mapping; Nora to add compliance bullets. target RFC review in 2 days.

Rob: will start RFC PR today. tagging @Jia for cache/versioning sync tomorrow.

